$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: safekeys/distribution
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-28"
summary: >-
  Name reservation and release pipeline for the 0.1.0 publish: npm under @smithgray (safekeys-mcp,
  safekeys-sdk, safekeys-cli with per-platform binary packages — the esbuild pattern, no lifecycle
  scripts, no install-time downloads) with defensive unscoped placeholders; PyPI safekeys plus
  safekeys-mcp/safe-keys placeholders via Trusted Publishing; Docker Hub org safekeys with GHCR
  canonical mirror, multi-arch, cosign-signed and SBOM-attested; crates.io placeholder crate; Go
  binaries with checksums and SBOM on the GitHub Release; Homebrew formula prepared for a future
  tap. Every publish job is gated on a release environment requiring James's approval, and every
  artefact is verifiable by the installer (cosign verify, npm audit signatures, sha256 checksums).
  No agent publishes anything: first publishes are human acts listed in docs/RELEASING.md.
$system: safekeys/system
$service: safekeys/system
intent: >-
  The MVP is real but unnameable: the npm org, PyPI names, Docker org, and crates.io name are either
  unclaimed, unprepared, or — in one case — squatted by a third party. Reserving the official and
  defensive names and building a gated, provenance-carrying release pipeline is what lets 0.1.0 ship
  without a rename later and without shipping under an impersonatable identity.
status: built
flows:
  - id: tag-triggered-release
    name: Tag-triggered release pipeline
    steps:
      - action: receive
        id: s1
        target: A v* tag is pushed.
      - action: validate
        id: s2
        target: >-
          Build, full test suite, and the red-team job run first; any failure stops the release
          before a publish job starts.
      - action: send
        id: s3
        target: >-
          Publish jobs run against the release environment: npm with provenance (the meta package
          and every per-platform binary package), PyPI via Trusted Publishing, images signed and
          attested to GHCR and Docker Hub, binaries and checksums and SBOM attached to the Release,
          Homebrew formula updated.
      - action: observe
        id: s4
        target: >-
          Nothing ships without James's environment approval; every artefact carries a digest or
          provenance record.
  - id: reserve-names
    name: Reserve the defensive names
    steps:
      - action: setup
        id: s1
        target: >-
          Placeholder packages are prepared: npm safekeys-mcp and safekeys-cli (0.0.1, README only),
          PyPI safekeys-mcp and safe-keys (0.0.1, README only), crates.io safekeys (0.0.1, doc
          comment only).
      - action: observe
        id: s2
        target: >-
          Each placeholder packs cleanly with no code inside (npm pack --dry-run, python -m build,
          cargo package --list).
      - action: record
        id: s3
        target: >-
          James executes the one-time account and org creation steps from docs/RELEASING.md and
          performs every first publish himself.
  - id: verify-install
    name: Verify your install
    steps:
      - action: receive
        id: s1
        target: An operator has installed an artefact — an image, an npm package, or a release binary.
      - action: validate
        id: s2
        target: >-
          Container images verify with cosign keyless, pinned to the repo's workflow identity
          (https://github.com/Smith-Gray-Pty-Ltd/safekeys/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/v*);
          npm provenance verifies with npm audit signatures; release binaries verify against the
          sha256 checksums on the GitHub Release.
      - action: observe
        id: s3
        target: >-
          The verify commands are documented on the security page of the docs site and in
          SECURITY.md, and fail loudly when the identity or checksum does not match.
contracts:
  - description: >-
      Every distributable carries a name that is either owned by Smith & Gray or defensively
      reserved, and documentation names only the official channels.
    id: official-names-only
    must_have:
      - npm packages publish under @smithgray: safekeys-mcp, safekeys-sdk, safekeys-cli
      - >-
        The CLI meta package @smithgray/safekeys-cli resolves the prebuilt binary through
        optionalDependencies on per-platform packages (@smithgray/safekeys-cli-darwin-arm64,
        -darwin-x64, -linux-x64, -linux-arm64, -win32-x64 as applicable) — the esbuild pattern: no
        download at install time and no lifecycle scripts
      - >-
        Unscoped npm safekeys-mcp and safekeys-cli exist as 0.0.1 placeholders with no code and no
        install scripts
      - >-
        PyPI safekeys is the real SDK; safekeys-mcp and safe-keys are 0.0.1 placeholders pointing at
        safekeys
      - >-
        Docker images publish as safekeys/sidecar and safekeys/control-plane with
        ghcr.io/smith-gray-pty-ltd/safekeys-* as the canonical mirror
      - A crates.io safekeys 0.0.1 placeholder crate reserves the Rust name
      - >-
        Docs and install snippets reference only official names and never the unscoped npm safekeys
        package owned by a third party
      - SECURITY.md states the official package names so impersonation is detectable
  - description: >-
      Publishing is gated, reproducible, and provenance-carrying; nothing ships without a human
      click.
    id: gated-release-pipeline
    must_have:
      - A v* tag runs build, the full test suite, and the red-team job before any publish job
      - Every publish job targets a GitHub release environment whose required reviewer is James
      - >-
        npm packages carry publishConfig access public and provenance true, published via npm
        provenance from Actions OIDC — including every per-platform CLI package
      - PyPI publishes via Trusted Publishing (OIDC) with no stored API tokens
      - >-
        Container images are multi-arch (amd64, arm64), signed with cosign keyless and accompanied
        by an SBOM attestation, pushed to GHCR as canonical and Docker Hub as mirror
      - >-
        Docker Hub authenticates with a scoped access token (push on the safekeys org only) held in
        the release environment, the one registry without an OIDC option
      - >-
        Go binaries, checksums, and SBOMs attach to the GitHub Release; a Homebrew formula update is
        generated for a future tap
      - A dry-run mode exercises the whole pipeline without publishing
  - description: >-
      Installers can verify what they installed, and the verification commands are documented where
      operators will look for them.
    id: verify-your-install
    must_have:
      - >-
        Container images verify with cosign keyless, pinned to the repository's workflow identity,
        with the command documented for both GHCR and Docker Hub
      - npm packages expose provenance and the docs say npm audit signatures to confirm it
      - Release binaries come with sha256 checksums and a documented verification command
      - >-
        The verify commands are in SECURITY.md and on the docs security page, and fail loudly when
        identity or checksum does not match
  - description: >-
      The reservation surface is documented so a human can execute the one-time and per-release
      steps.
    id: release-runbook
    must_have:
      - >-
        docs/RELEASING.md lists every reserved name with registry, name, status, owner account, and
        publisher — including every per-platform CLI binary package
      - >-
        The one-time human steps (org creation, trusted publishing, environment, tap repo) are
        listed in order and marked as James's actions
      - Per-release steps are listed separately
      - The empty npm org hold for @safekeys/* is noted: create it, publish nothing
      - >-
        A vanity Go module path (safekeys.ai/go) is recorded as a future TODO in the site spec, not
        switched now
  - description: The packages themselves are hardened against supply-chain abuse.
    id: package-hygiene
    must_have:
      - No postinstall or any lifecycle scripts in any npm package
      - Minimal runtime dependencies in every real package
      - npm 2FA required for the smithgray org and PyPI 2FA on the accounts that publish
      - >-
        Versions align at 0.1.0 across Go, npm, and PyPI with a CHANGELOG.md entry covering
        output-control and dev-key-custody
      - README and package descriptions mark 0.1.0 as early/preview
      - >-
        A curl | sh fallback for the CLI documents downloading the GitHub Release binary and
        verifying its sha256 checksum; it is secondary to the npm packages
tests:
  - contracts:
      - official-names-only
    expect:
      - assertion: >-
          no package.json, pyproject.toml, or doc names the unscoped npm safekeys package as an
          install target
      - assertion: placeholder packages pack cleanly with no code
    flow: reserve-names
    id: placeholder-packages-pack-clean
    setup:
      packages:
        - "npm: safekeys-mcp, safekeys-cli"
        - "pypi: safekeys-mcp, safe-keys"
        - "crates: safekeys"
  - contracts:
      - official-names-only
    expect:
      - assertion: >-
          the meta package resolves the platform binary through optionalDependencies with no
          lifecycle script
      - assertion: a wrong-platform resolution fails loudly rather than falling back to a download
    flow: verify-install
    id: cli-meta-resolves-platform-binary
    setup:
      pattern: esbuild
  - contracts:
      - gated-release-pipeline
    expect:
      - assertion: the release workflow passes in dry-run mode on a test tag
      - assertion: no publish job runs outside the release environment
      - assertion: >-
          no long-lived registry token is stored in repo secrets except the scoped Docker Hub token
          in the release environment
    flow: tag-triggered-release
    id: pipeline-dry-run-green
    setup:
      mode: dry-run
      tag: v0.0.0-test
  - contracts:
      - release-runbook
    expect:
      - assertion: docs/RELEASING.md contains the name table and the ordered one-time human steps
      - assertion: per-release steps are listed separately
    flow: reserve-names
    id: runbook-lists-james-steps
    setup:
      doc: docs/RELEASING.md
  - contracts:
      - package-hygiene
    expect:
      - assertion: no npm package declares a postinstall or any lifecycle script
      - assertion: versions align at 0.1.0 and CHANGELOG.md covers output-control and dev-key-custody
    flow: reserve-names
    id: hygiene-checks-pass
    setup:
      static_check: true
  - contracts:
      - verify-your-install
    expect:
      - assertion: >-
          the documented cosign command pins the repo's workflow identity and verifies a published
          image
      - assertion: npm audit signatures confirm provenance for a published package
      - assertion: sha256 checksums match a downloaded release binary
    flow: verify-install
    id: verify-commands-documented-and-correct
    setup:
      doc: SECURITY.md + docs security page
decisions:
  - id: esbuild-pattern-for-cli
    decision: >-
      Distribute the CLI as @smithgray/safekeys-cli with optionalDependencies on per-platform
      packages containing the prebuilt binary.
    rationale: |-
      No download at install time, no lifecycle scripts, checksummed by the npm registry itself,
      and npm installs exactly the one platform package the host needs. This is the pattern esbuild
      and Rollup use; it survives air-gapped installs, mirrors, and --ignore-scripts better than
      any installer script would.
    consequences: |-
      Five platform packages join the name table and the gated publish job. A test asserts the meta
      package resolves the right binary per platform and fails loudly on an unsupported platform.
      The curl | sh path remains, documented as secondary, and verifies a sha256 checksum.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
  - id: ghcr-canonical-dockerhub-mirror
    decision: >-
      Publish images to ghcr.io/smith-gray-pty-ltd as canonical and Docker Hub org safekeys as a
      mirror.
    rationale: |-
      GHCR requires no additional credential (GITHUB_TOKEN suffices) and keeps the image next to the
      code and its provenance; Docker Hub is where operators search. Deployments pin GHCR digests.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
  - id: no-agent-publishes
    decision: >-
      Every first publish and every account/org creation is performed by James; agents prepare
      packages, workflows, and the runbook only.
    rationale: |-
      First publishes create registry identities that cannot be renamed, and account creation is a
      human act of authority. The pipeline is gated on a release environment with a required human
      reviewer for the same reason — nothing ships without a click.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-28"
implementation:
  primary: .github/workflows/release.yml
  test_code: packages/cli/test/resolve.test.mjs
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - safekeys/output-control
  - safekeys/deployment-packaging
  - safekeys/docs-hosting
